finding: Streaming violates the unit's allocation-free warm turnover premise: syncBuffers calls registerDraws on every mesh membership version change (and args/view growth or alpha-key change); registerDraws calls rebuildMeshCounts, which destroys and recreates two buffers and one bind group for EVERY count-bearing mesh. A counted invocation of the actual rebuild function with 200 meshes created 400 buffers/2400 bytes at warm and another 40000 buffers/240000 bytes plus 20000 bind groups across 100 same-size handle replacements. The unchanged-scene allocation oracle cannot detect this. Retain bounded reusable count state across turnover, ideally write draw-record indexCount words directly rather than dispatch/copy scratch words; add a warm count-bearing remove/reuse allocation gate. Validation is also non-atomic and incomplete: invalid sources are accepted by Meshes.add, then rebuild destroys old entries and can throw after syncBuffers has already consumed meshes.version, leaving the next frame to bypass rebuilding with partial state. NaN offset passes every current predicate and is silently converted to word offset zero (independently reproduced). Validate finite aligned in-range offsets before registry mutation, and publish rebuild/version state only after successful preparation. Seven focused GPU-count/handle tests pass, but cover neither failure. Linux evidence is applicable: 61d9003 and HEAD have identical tree 4db69468f6a63bc0c2bb55b8b65915a8eb74a021.
